# Modules across files: `import` and `using`.
#
#   ./bin/crystal run samples/iyi/modules.iyi
#
# Everything below compiles and runs today. `hello.iyi` covers traits and
# `impl` in a single file; this one covers the part a single file cannot show.

module samples/modules

# R-1: `import` names a module and loads it at most once, so the import graph
# is a DAG rather than textual inclusion. It brings the module into scope
# under its qualified name and nothing more — after this line, and with no
# `using`, the module's names are reachable only as `App::Greeter::Greet` and
# `App::Greeter.polite`.
import app/greeter
import app/formal

# II.3: `using` is what makes those names available unqualified. It is written
# by the consumer, not performed by the library: importing a module never
# changes what the importer's unqualified names mean, so a library cannot
# capture a name in your file by adding one to itself.
using app/greeter

# The selective form narrows a `using` to the names listed. Here it is not
# decoration — `app/greeter` and `app/formal` both export `title`, and taking
# both whole would make every unqualified `title` in this module ambiguous.
using app/formal::{address}

pub struct User
  getter name : String

  def initialize(@name : String)
  end
end

# `Greet` resolves through the `using` above, so `impl` needs no qualification.
#
# R-3's orphan rule allows this impl here because `User` is declared here:
# this is the type's module. It would equally be allowed in `app/greeter`,
# the trait's module. Nowhere else — a third module that imports both is
# refused, which is what makes two modules unable to define the same impl.
impl Greet for User
  def greet : String
    # `polite` comes from `app/greeter`, `address` from `app/formal`, and
    # `shout` is this module's own function, declared below. All three are
    # written the same way, because to this file they are all just names.
    shout(polite(name)) + " " + address(name)
  end
end

# A module's own functions are in scope for the types declared in it, without
# `self.` and without qualification: an iyi module is a compilation unit, and
# these are its functions. Not exported, so nothing outside can call it.
def shout(text : String) : String
  text.upcase
end

puts User.new("iyi").greet

# `title` was reachable unqualified from neither `using`: `app/greeter`'s was
# left ambiguous by nothing (only one `using` was whole), and `app/formal`'s
# was excluded by the selective form. Qualification always works.
puts App::Greeter.title
puts App::Formal.title

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What this sample would report as an error
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# * Widening the second directive to `using app/formal` and then writing an
#   unqualified `title` is an error, and it is reported at the `title`, not at
#   the `using`. Widening it alone is not an error at all: two modules
#   exporting the same name is not by itself a mistake, so nothing is wrong
#   until a name is written that could mean either (SPEC.md II.3).
#
# * Adding `def title` here makes even that legal again. A local definition
#   beats a used one, silently, which is what keeps a library from breaking
#   its consumers by adding an export.
#
# * Moving the `impl` above into a module that is neither `app/greeter` nor
#   this one is refused by R-3, naming both modules where it would be allowed.
#
# * `using app/greeter::{greeting}` — `greeting` is declared there without
#   `pub`, so it is not part of the module's surface. The error names it at the
#   `using`, where the author can see which of the names they asked for they
#   cannot have. Widening to a bare `using app/greeter` does not reach it
#   either; it simply is not there to be found (R-2b).
#
# * `App::Greeter.greeting` — qualifying it does not get around `pub` either.
#   It has to be unreachable, not merely absent from `using`: `.iyimod` carries
#   a module's exports and nothing else, so if another module could call an
#   unmarked name, that metadata would not be enough to compile against
#   (SPEC.md IV.2).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Not yet implemented
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# * `module app/greeter` is declared in one notation and referred to in
#   another (`App::Greeter`). SPEC.md IV.6 #6 is where that gets settled, and
#   0.1.0 settles it — it is in every line of user code, so it cannot be
#   changed once there is user code.
#
# * Each imported module is still re-analysed from source. `.iyimod` — reading
#   a module's exported signatures instead of its body — is the whole point of
#   R-1 and is not built yet.
